=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the iptables FORWARD chain on router6. The chain contains three catch-all DROP rules (rules 2, 3, 4) that discard all TCP, UDP, and ICMP traffic arriving on eth2 — the r6_r3 interface connecting router6 to router3 and the wider WAN. Only a single narrow ACCEPT rule (rule 1) exists, permitting TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1; everything else from eth2 is silently dropped.

The mechanism is a one-way barrier. Traffic leaving the mswitch2 zone (172.18.50.0/24, 172.18.51.0/24) toward the WAN exits router6 on eth2 unimpeded — those packets arrive on eth1/eth0 and the FORWARD chain has no rules blocking that direction. However, any traffic arriving from the router3 side on eth2 that needs to be forwarded inward to mswitch2 clients is dropped. This breaks communication symmetrically: packets from PC11–PC14 destined for PC15–PC18 and laptop4/5 arrive at router6 eth2 and are dropped immediately; packets originating from PC15–PC18 can reach PC11–PC14, but PC11's ICMP/TCP replies return via router4 → router3 → router6 eth2 and are silently discarded there, so no application-layer exchange completes.

The fault is isolated to router6's iptables FORWARD chain, rules 2–4. Router4 and the 192.168.200.0/24 network are fully functional; PC11 can reach its own gateway (192.168.200.1), router5, and the entire Location A zone via router5 → router0. Only destinations routed through router6 (172.18.50.x and 172.18.51.x) are unreachable.

Root cause: router6 has three overly broad iptables FORWARD DROP rules blocking all protocol traffic inbound on eth2, severing transit between the mswitch2 zone and the rest of the network.

Corrective fix: remove rules 2, 3, and 4 from router6's FORWARD chain in reverse order to preserve numbering: `iptables -D FORWARD 4; iptables -D FORWARD 3; iptables -D FORWARD 2`. If a firewall policy is required, replace with stateful rules using `-m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT` to permit return traffic.
=== END ===